# Verifying changes

Run `make check` (lints, runs the headless tests) before reporting work as done.
Add a case to `tests/smoke.js` for any new logic in `lib/`; it runs under plain
`gjs` against a fake daemon on a temporary socket, so it must stay free of side
effects and never connect to the real voxtype socket.

## You cannot restart GNOME Shell

The session is Wayland, so the shell cannot be restarted in place and
`gnome-extensions list` will not show a newly linked extension until the user
logs out and back in. Never claim the extension was loaded or enabled without
evidence. To exercise shell-side code, use `make nested` (GNOME 49+:
`gnome-shell --devkit --wayland`; requires `mutter-dev-bin` on Ubuntu), and
tell the user when a logout is genuinely required.

`make nested` uses `GSETTINGS_BACKEND=memory`. Do not drop that: the nested
shell shares dconf with the real session, so enabling the extension there would
otherwise enable it in the real session too.

`make logs` follows `gnome-shell` output for runtime errors.

## Do not disturb the user's voxtype

The user dictates with voxtype while you work. Never restart, stop or
reconfigure the voxtype daemon to test this extension; the nested shell reads
the same socket, so a real recording is enough to see the overlay.

## Do not edit generated files

`node_modules/` is build output and is ignored by git.
